A colleague writes: "Share B's daily returns have a flat ACF, so they are independent. That means a normal distribution with the 250-day sample sd is all we need for its risk. On day 1,404 that sd gives 19.3% a year, so our position limits are set from it."

Write a reply to the risk committee (about 250 words). Cover:

(a) what the flat ACF does and does not show;

(b) the test you would run instead, and its result on B;

(c) what B's tails say about a normal model, with numbers;

(d) what is wrong with a 250-day sd on day 1,404, and what you would use instead;

(e) one thing these numbers cannot tell you yet.
